  "facts": [
    "The Washington Bridge’s original 1930 southern span, a 15‑ft‑high stone‑façade bascule bridge, was designed by Clarence W. Hudson and Carl L. Otto and dedicated to George Washington for his historic crossing with General Rochambeau.",
    "In 1968, a controversial pre‑stressed concrete “false arch” westbound span replaced the original bascule, a rare construction method that later proved structurally fragile, leading to the bridge’s abrupt 2023 closure.",
    "The pedestrian and bike pathway, now part of the Washington‑Rochambeau National Historic Trail, was transformed into the 2015 George Redman Linear Park, featuring an 11‑foot bike lane, stamped‑concrete footpath, and scenic river overlooks."
  ],
